(Inspire People: A Natural English Collocation) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In this article ? Meaning and use) Tj
T*
(? Bilingual examples) Tj
T*
(? Rules and common mistakes) Tj
T*
(? Practice) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Meaning and use) Tj
T*
(To inspire someone is to give them enthusiasm, confidence, ideas, or motivation. Inspire) Tj
T*
(people is a natural collocation in leadership, education, creativity, and everyday English.) Tj
T*
(A very useful pattern is inspire + person + to + base verb: The teacher inspired me to study) Tj
T*
(languages.) Tj
T*
(2. Bilingual examples) Tj
T*
(Her story inspired many people.) Tj
T*
(Su historia inspiró a muchas personas.) Tj
T*
(The teacher inspired me to study languages.) Tj
T*
(El profesor me inspiró a estudiar idiomas.) Tj
T*
(Great leaders inspire people to exceed their own expectations.) Tj
T*
(Los grandes líderes inspiran a las personas a superar sus propias expectativas.) Tj
T*
(3. Rules and common mistakes) Tj
T*
(In more formal English, inspire can combine with nouns such as confidence, hope, and) Tj
T*
(loyalty. Adverbs such as deeply or truly can add emphasis when needed.) Tj
T*
(Do not confuse inspire with motivate in every context. They overlap, but inspire often) Tj
T*
(suggests creating enthusiasm, ideas, or a sense of possibility.) Tj
T*
(Study tip: Read the English sentence aloud first, then check the Spanish meaning.) Tj
T*
(4. Practice) Tj
T*
(? Complete: His example inspired us ___ keep going.) Tj
T*
(? Translate: Su creatividad inspira a la gente.) Tj
T*
(Answers: to · Her creativity inspires people. - Su creatividad inspira a la gente. - Su) Tj
T*
(creatividad inspira a la gente.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
