T*
(1. Meaning and use) Tj
T*
(Might is a modal verb for possibility and uncertainty. It does not encode a fixed) Tj
T*
(percentage; context tells us how likely the speaker considers the event.) Tj
T*
(The form is might + base verb. There is no third-person -s and no to: He might come - Puede) Tj
T*
(que venga; she might need help - Puede que necesite ayuda.) Tj
T*
(2. Bilingual examples) Tj
T*
(Take an umbrella; it might rain later.) Tj
T*
(Lleva un paraguas; puede que llueva más tarde.) Tj
T*
(She might be at work.) Tj
T*
(Puede que ella esté en el trabajo.) Tj
T*
(The shop might not be open.) Tj
T*
(Puede que la tienda no esté abierta.) Tj
T*
(3. Rules and common mistakes) Tj
T*
(Use might not + base verb for a possible negative situation: I might not go out tonight -) Tj
T*
(Puede que no salga esta noche.) Tj
T*
(Avoid "might to go" and "might goes". Like other central modal verbs, might is followed) Tj
T*
(directly by the base form.) Tj
T*
(Study tip: Read the English sentence aloud first. Try to explain it before checking the) Tj
T*
(Spanish translation.) Tj
T*
