(1. Why thinking first matters) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Speaking quickly is not always the same as speaking well. In a conversation, a short pause) Tj
T*
(can help you understand what you really want to say, choose a better tone, and avoid words) Tj
T*
(you may regret later.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(In English, you do not need to answer immediately. Native and fluent speakers often use) Tj
T*
(short expressions to create thinking time. These phrases can make you sound calmer and more) Tj
T*
(natural, not less confident.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Main idea: A pause is not an empty space. It can be a moment to choose a clearer, kinder and) Tj
T*
(more useful response.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. Useful phrases to give yourself time) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(These expressions are useful when you need a few seconds before answering.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let me think for a moment.) Tj
T*
(Déjame pensar un momento.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(That's a good question.) Tj
T*
(Es una buena pregunta.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(I'm not sure how to put it.) Tj
T*
(No estoy seguro/a de cómo expresarlo.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(What I mean is...) Tj
T*
(Lo que quiero decir es...) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Let me rephrase that.) Tj
T*
(Déjame decirlo de otra manera.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(I need a second to think about that.) Tj
T*
(Necesito un segundo para pensar en eso.) Tj
T*
