T*
() Tj
T*
(1. Meaning and use) Tj
T*
(To be honest introduces a frank or personal comment. It often signals that the speaker is) Tj
T*
(about to say what they genuinely think or feel.) Tj
T*
(It commonly appears at the beginning followed by a comma, but it can also appear later: I) Tj
T*
(don't like it, to be honest.) Tj
T*
(2. Bilingual examples) Tj
T*
(To be honest, I found the first module difficult.) Tj
T*
(Sinceramente, el primer módulo me pareció difícil.) Tj
T*
(To be honest, I'd rather stay home.) Tj
T*
(La verdad, preferiría quedarme en casa.) Tj
T*
(I don't like it, to be honest.) Tj
T*
(No me gusta, la verdad.) Tj
T*
(3. Rules and common mistakes) Tj
T*
(It is natural in speech and informal or semi-formal writing. In a formal report or academic) Tj
T*
(essay, more neutral phrasing is usually preferable.) Tj
T*
(Avoid using it at the start of every answer. Repetition can sound mechanical and may) Tj
T*
(accidentally imply that other statements are less honest.) Tj
T*
(Study tip: Say the English example aloud before checking the Spanish translation.) Tj
T*
